### 🏗️ Semantic HTML Elements vs Div Elements

**AVOID** semantic HTML elements that can have default browser styles:

```tsx
// ❌ WRONG: Semantic HTML elements with potential conflicts
<section className="mb-12 p-8 bg-card rounded-lg border border-border">
<header className="text-center mb-12">
<main className="max-w-5xl mx-auto p-8">
<article className="mb-12 p-8 bg-card rounded-lg">
<aside className="flex gap-4 flex-wrap mb-4">
<footer className="text-center mb-12">
<nav className="flex gap-4 flex-wrap mb-4">

// ✅ CORRECT: Use div elements for consistent styling
<div className="mb-12 p-8 bg-card rounded-lg border border-border">
<div className="text-center mb-12">
<div className="max-w-5xl mx-auto p-8">
<div className="mb-12 p-8 bg-card rounded-lg">
<div className="flex gap-4 flex-wrap mb-4">
<div className="text-center mb-12">
<div className="flex gap-4 flex-wrap mb-4">
```

**Why avoid semantic HTML elements:**

- **Default Browser Styles**: `<section>`, `<header>`, `<main>`, etc. have built-in browser styles
- **CSS Specificity**: Browser defaults can override Tailwind classes
- **Inconsistent Behavior**: Different browsers may have different default styles
- **Maintenance Issues**: Harder to predict styling behavior across browsers

### 🎯 Benefits of Tailwind Approach

1. **✅ Consistency**: All styling uses the same system
2. **✅ Maintainability**: No style objects to manage
3. **✅ Performance**: No inline style calculations
4. **✅ Theme Integration**: Automatic design system colors
5. **✅ Responsive**: Built-in responsive utilities
6. **✅ Clean Code**: More readable and maintainable
7. **✅ Bundle Size**: Smaller CSS output
8. **✅ Developer Experience**: IntelliSense and autocomplete

## 🎯 Key Takeaways

1. **Single Component**: Create one flexible component, not multiple specific ones
2. **Tailwind Classes**: Use `text-xs`, `text-sm`, `text-lg`, etc. instead of semantic HTML elements
3. **No Semantic HTML**: Use `div` instead of `h1`, `h2`, `section`, `header`, etc. to avoid style conflicts
4. **No Inline Styles**: Use Tailwind classes instead of `style={{}}` syntax
5. **No Style Objects**: Avoid creating style objects, use Tailwind classes directly
6. **Global Layout**: Put shared components (header/footer) in main layout
7. **Type Safety**: Always define TypeScript interfaces for props
8. **Theme Compatibility**: Use only approved Modus CSS variables
9. **Event Handling**: Use proper callback patterns with useCallback
10. **Icon Integration**: Follow consistent icon spacing patterns with Tailwind
11. **Performance**: Prioritize bundle size and compilation speed
12. **Styling Separation**: Tailwind for typography/layout, design system for colors

---

**Remember**: These patterns ensure your Modus Web Components are shareable, maintainable, and performant across Next.js applications. Follow these guidelines to avoid common pitfalls and create production-ready components.
